---
import Layout from '../layouts/Layout.astro';
import Header from '../components/Header.astro';
import Footer from '../components/Footer.astro';
import JsonLd from '../components/seo/JsonLd.astro';
import { useTranslations } from '../i18n/utils';

const locale = 'en' as const;
const t = useTranslations(locale);

const articleSchema = {
  '@context': 'https://schema.org',
  '@type': 'Article',
  headline: t('buying.title'),
  description: t('buying.description'),
  inLanguage: 'en-US',
  author: { '@type': 'Person', name: 'Andy' },
};
---
<Layout
  title={t('buying.title')}
  description={t('buying.description')}
  lang={locale}
  type="article"
>
  <JsonLd slot="head" schema={articleSchema} />
  <Header locale={locale} />

  <main id="main" tabindex="-1" class="max-w-3xl mx-auto px-6 py-16">
    <h1 class="font-serif text-text-primary mb-4" style="font-size: var(--text-h1); line-height: var(--text-h1--line-height);">
      {t('buying.title')}
    </h1>
    <p class="text-text-secondary text-lg mb-12">{t('buying.subtitle')}</p>

    <div class="prose">
      <p>
        The gim aisle at a Korean market looks like a wall of nearly identical
        black-and-green packets. Most of the time the differences come down to
        three things: <strong>what form it's in</strong>, <strong>whether
        it's seasoned</strong>, and <strong>which oil they used</strong>.
        This page is the short version — enough to walk into H Mart, pick a
        pack with intent, and leave in five minutes.
      </p>

      <div class="aspect-[16/9] rounded-md bg-seafoam-light/60 flex items-center justify-center text-text-muted text-sm border border-dashed border-kelp/30 my-8">
        Image — packaged gim on a Korean market shelf (to be added)
      </div>

      <h2>What to buy first</h2>
      <p>
        If this is your first pack, narrow the wall down to three formats —
        each does one thing well, and together they cover most of how Koreans
        actually eat gim:
      </p>
      <ul>
        <li>
          <strong>Dosirak-gim (도시락김).</strong> Small, pre-cut, single-serve
          foil packs of seasoned roasted laver. Tear, eat, done. The single
          easiest entry point. Most kids in Korea know gim through this format
          first.
        </li>
        <li>
          <strong>Kim-jaban (김자반).</strong> Crumbled, seasoned flakes in a
          bottle or pouch — usually with sesame and a small amount of sugar.
          Sprinkle a spoonful on a bowl of rice and you have a meal. Keeps for
          weeks once opened, unlike sheets.
        </li>
        <li>
          <strong>Gimbap-gim (김밥김).</strong> Full-size unseasoned sheets,
          built for rolling. Skip this until you're ready to cook, then jump
          to the <a href="/articles/gimbap-basics">Gimbap Basics</a> recipe.
        </li>
      </ul>
      <p>
        The seasoned full sheets (<em>kim-gui</em>) and the deep-fried snack
        (<em>kim-bugak</em>) are great too, but they aren't the place to
        start. The three above will tell you whether you actually like gim
        before you commit to a stack of full sheets.
      </p>

      <h2>How to read the label</h2>
      <p>
        Korean labels are mostly in Hangul, but the four things that matter
        are easy to spot once you know what you're looking at.
      </p>
      <ul>
        <li>
          <strong>Seasoned vs. unseasoned.</strong> <em>조미김</em> is
          seasoned (oil + salt, ready to eat). <em>무조미김</em> or just
          plain <em>김</em> with no roasting language is unseasoned — the
          format you want for cooking. If the front of the pack is glossy and
          dotted with salt, it's seasoned.
        </li>
        <li>
          <strong>Which oil.</strong> Look at the ingredients list:
          <em>참기름</em> (sesame), <em>들기름</em> (perilla), or a generic
          <em>식용유</em> (vegetable oil). Sesame is the classic, mild and
          nutty. Perilla is grassier and slightly more savory — a Korean
          favorite. Generic vegetable oil is what you usually find in the
          cheapest packs and tends to taste flat. The oil is most of the
          flavor in seasoned gim, so this matters more than the brand.
        </li>
        <li>
          <strong>How salty.</strong> Salt content varies more than the
          packaging suggests. Two seasoned packs from the same shelf can sit
          a full grade apart. The <a href="/reviews">Reviews page</a> rates
          every gim we've opened on a 0–3 scale for both oiliness and
          saltiness, so you can sort for the profile you want.
        </li>
        <li>
          <strong>Origin.</strong> Most Korean gim comes from one of three
          coastal regions — <em>Wando (완도)</em>, <em>Sinan (신안)</em>, or
          <em>Goheung (고흥)</em>. All three produce well-regarded laver;
          you don't need to chase a specific one. It's mostly a marker that
          the product is genuinely Korean rather than re-packed elsewhere.
        </li>
      </ul>

      <h2>Where to buy</h2>
      <p>
        In the United States, the path of least resistance is a Korean
        grocery chain — <strong>H Mart</strong> and <strong>Hannam
        Chain</strong> stock the widest range and rotate fresh inventory.
        Most metro areas have at least one branch, and the gim aisle is
        usually near the dried goods or the side-dish refrigerator.
      </p>
      <p>
        For online, three options cover most cases: <strong>Amazon</strong>
        (fast, but the selection skews toward Korean-American brands like
        Nori or CJ rather than the kinds of gim you'd find on a Korean
        shelf), <strong>Weee!</strong> (closer to a real Korean-mart
        selection, ships chilled and frozen), and <strong>HMart.com</strong>
        (the in-store catalog, online). For specific Korean brands that
        aren't carried locally, the Korean direct-export sites — Coupang
        Global and Gmarket Global — work, but shipping eats into the price.
      </p>

      <h2>Price range</h2>
      <p>
        Rough US prices in 2026, useful as a sanity check:
      </p>
      <ul>
        <li><strong>Dosirak-gim, 8-pack:</strong> $5–8.</li>
        <li><strong>Kim-jaban, 50–80g jar or pouch:</strong> $4–7.</li>
        <li><strong>Gimbap-gim, 10 full sheets:</strong> $4–6.</li>
        <li><strong>Premium seasoned full sheets (kim-gui):</strong> $7–12 for a stack.</li>
      </ul>
      <p>
        If you see a dosirak pack for under $3 or a kim-jaban for under $3,
        check the oil. Cheap packs almost always use generic vegetable oil
        and lean heavily on salt to compensate. Not necessarily bad, but
        worth knowing what you're trading off.
      </p>

      <h2>Storage</h2>
      <p>
        Sealed, gim keeps for the better part of a year — moisture is the
        enemy, not time. Once you open a pack, it goes soggy in a day or two
        if left out. The fix is simple: reseal tightly, push the air out, and
        keep it in a dry cupboard. For long-term storage, a zip bag in the
        freezer works fine; thaw the sheets at room temperature for a few
        minutes before eating.
      </p>
      <p>
        If a sheet has lost its snap, drag it across a dry pan over low heat
        for three or four seconds per side. The crispness comes back almost
        instantly.
      </p>

      <h2>Where to go next</h2>
      <p>
        Once you have a pack at home, the next two stops:
        <a href="/reviews">Reviews</a> sorts the gim we've actually opened by
        oiliness, saltiness, and overall rating, so you can pick the next
        pack with more intent. <a href="/articles/gimbap-basics">Gimbap
        Basics</a> walks through the rolling method when you're ready to
        cook. Or back up a step to <a href="/what-is-gim">What is Korean
        Gim?</a> for the longer story behind the product.
      </p>
    </div>
  </main>

  <Footer locale={locale} />
</Layout>
